h\") gemma4-dict\ntool-list-dir ::= (\"list_dir\") gemma4-dict\ntool-read-file ::= (\"read_file\") gemma4-dict\ntool-write-file ::= (\"write_file\") gemma4-dict\nuntil-28 ::= | [<] until-28-01 | [^<] until-28\nuntil-28-01 ::= | [<] until-28-01 | [|] until-28-02 | [^<|] until-28\nuntil-28-02 ::= | [<] until-28-01 | [\"] until-28-03 | [^\"<] until-28\nuntil-28-03 ::= | [<] until-28-01 | [|] until-28-04 | [^<|] until-28\nuntil-28-04 ::= | [<] until-28-01 | [^<>] until-28\n","grammar_lazy":true,"grammar_triggers":[{"type":0,"value":"<|tool_call>","token":48}],"preserved_tokens":[48,49,100,101,105],"chat_format":"peg-gemma4","reasoning_format":"deepseek","reasoning_in_content":false,"generation_prompt":"<|channel>thought\n","samplers":["penalties","dry","top_n_sigma","top_k","typ_p","top_p","min_p","xtc","temperature"],"speculative.types":"none","timings_per_token":false,"post_sampling_probs":false,"backend_sampling":false,"lora":[]},"next_token":[{"has_next_token":false,"has_new_line":false,"n_remain":-1,"n_decoded":0}],"prompt":"<bos><|turn>system\n<|think|>\nYou are a software engineer. Your workspace directory is /tmp/runs/smokes/gemma-q4/kanban-board/no_verification/runs/201/app. Implement the task described by the user by creating files in the workspace using the provided tools. Work until the task is complete, then call finish. Use only the tools provided to you.\n\nEnvironment: the tools listed in this request are the complete set available to you. Tools not listed do not exist in this environment and cannot be installed or obtained. Review what is available and use whatever helps you complete and check your work. If execution tools are provided, they give you a live development environment: the commands you run through them execute for real in your workspace, on a machine with Node.js and npm installed and the npm registry reachable, so the packages you install and the servers you start are real. If no installation or execution tools are provided, author every required file completely from first principles; dependencies declared in package.json files are provisioned by the environment after you finish.<|tool>declaration:read_file{description:<|\"|>Read a file in the workspace.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{path:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>path<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:write_file{description:<|\"|>Create or overwrite a file. Any missing parent directories in `path` are created automatically.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{content:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>},path:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>path<|\"|>,<|\"|>content<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:edit_file{description:<|\"|>Replace a unique substring in a file.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{new_str:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>},old_str:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>},path:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},required:[<|\"|>path<|\"|>,<|\"|>old_str<|\"|>,<|\"|>new_str<|\"|>],type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:list_dir{description:<|\"|>List a directory in the workspace.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{path:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><|tool>declaration:finish{description:<|\"|>Signal that the task is complete.<|\"|>,parameters:{properties:{summary:{type:<|\"|>STRING<|\"|>}},type:<|\"|>OBJECT<|\"|>}}<tool|><turn|>\n<|turn>user\n## Context\n\nWe are building a traditional client-server web application with real-time collaborative capabilities: a multi-user Kanban board. Multiple users view the same board and can create cards, move them between columns, and reorder them within a column via drag-and-drop. All connected clients must converge on the same board state in real time. To minimize external dependencies while retaining the power of PostgreSQL, we use PGLite embedded within a Node.js server, with Server-Sent Events (SSE) pushing updates to clients. Clients apply optimistic updates and reconcile against authoritative server state.\n\n## Goals / Non-Goals\n\n**Goals:**\n- A shared Kanban board with ordered columns, each containing an ordered list of cards.\n- Create cards, move cards across columns, and reorder cards within a column via drag-and-drop.\n- Persist board state durably on local disk using PGLite.\n- Broadcast every mutation to all connected clients so boards stay convergent in real time.\n- Optimistic UI: the dragging client updates immediately, then reconciles against the server's authoritative ordering.\n\n**Non-Goals:**\n- User authentication and authorization.\n- Multiple boards, labels, due dates, comments, or attachments.\n- Offline editing or CRDT-grade conflict resolution beyond last-write-wins convergence.\n\n## Decisions\n\n**Decision 1: Fractional position ordering**\n- *Rationale*: Each card carries a numeric `position` within its column. Inserting a card between two others uses a value between their positions, avoiding a full re-index on every move. This keeps moves cheap and ordering stable under concurrent edits.\n\n**Decision 2: Server-Sent Events (SSE) + HTTP mutations**\n- *Rationale*: We only need one-way real-time push (server → clients). Mutations are sent as standard HTTP requests. SSE is simple, native over HTTP, and avoids a WebSocket dependency.\n\n**Decision 3: Server is authoritative on ordering**\n- *Rationale*: Clients send intent (\"move card X into column C between A and B\"). The server computes the final canonical position, persists it atomically, and broadcasts the canonical result. Clients reconcile their optimistic guess against this canonical state.\n\n## Risks / Trade-offs\n\n- [Risk] Concurrent moves of the same card from two clients can diverge.\n  - *Mitigation*: Server-authoritative ordering plus broadcast of canonical state; clients replace their optimistic state with the server's on receipt.\n- [Risk] Fractional positions can collide or exhaust numeric precision after many inserts at the same spot.\n  - *Mitigation*: Detect collisions/exhaustion and renormalize the affected column's positions, then broadcast the corrected order.\n- [Risk] A card could momentarily appear in two columns during a cross-column move.\n  - *Mitigation*: Perform the move (remove from source, add to target) atomically in a single transaction; only broadcast committed state.\n\n## Why\n\nReal-time collaborative tools demand that every client converges on the same state despite concurrent edits. A Kanban board is a compact but demanding instance of this: it combines an interactive drag-and-drop UI with non-trivial ordering and concurrency requirements on the backend. It is an excellent demonstration of embedded PGLite plus SSE for a robust, lightweight, low-latency collaborative application without external infrastructure.\n\n## What Changes\n\n- We are introducing a brand new collaborative Kanban web application.\n- The backend is a Node.js server using embedded PGLite for raw SQL, persisting board state to local disk.\n- Real-time synchronization is handled via Server-Sent Events (SSE).\n- The frontend is a simple, lightweight Single Page Application (Vanilla JS via Vite) with drag-and-drop.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n### New Capabilities\n- `kanban-board`: ordered columns and cards, drag-and-drop move/reorder, real-time convergence via SSE, and optimistic client updates reconciled against server-authoritative ordering.\n\n### Modified Capabilities\nNone (Greenfield project).\n\n## Impact\n\nThis is a brand new project. It establishes the Node.js backend, the embedded PGLite schema for columns and cards, SSE connection handling, the ordering/concurrency logic, and the drag-and-drop frontend UI.\n\n## 1. Project Initialization\n\n- [ ] 1.1 Initialize the Node.js project and install backend dependencies (express, @electric-sql/pglite, cors)\n- [ ] 1.2 Initialize the Vanilla JS / Vite project for the frontend and install frontend dependencies\n- [ ] 1.3 Set up basic npm scripts for concurrently running backend and frontend development servers\n- [ ] 1.4 Make the app start from a fresh checkout with no manual steps: once the dependencies declared in its package.json files are installed, `npm run dev` at the project root starts both the backend and the frontend, the backend creates any directory it needs (such as PGLite's data directory), and the page the frontend serves reaches the backend's API and event stream\n\n## 2. Backend Infrastructure & Database\n\n- [ ] 2.1 Set up a basic Express server with CORS and JSON body parsing\n- [ ] 2.2 Initialize embedded PGLite configured to write to the local file system\n- [ ] 2.3 Create the database initialization script: a `columns` table (`id`, `title`, `position`) and a `cards` table (`id`, `column_id`, `text`, `position`, `created_at`). Seed default columns (e.g., \"To Do\", \"In Progress\", \"Done\")\n- [ ] 2.4 Create the board state endpoint (`GET /api/board`) returning all columns each with their cards ordered by `position`\n\n## 3. Board Mutations & Real-time Sync\n\n- [ ] 3.1 Implement `POST /api/cards` to create a card at the end of a column, persist it, and broadcast it\n- [ ] 3.2 Implement `PATCH /api/cards/:id/move` accepting `{ columnId, beforeId, afterId }`; the server computes the card's new `position` (between `afterId` and `beforeId`), updates `column_id` and `position` atomically in a transaction, and returns the canonical card\n- [ ] 3.3 Implement the SSE endpoint (`GET /api/stream`) that maintains active connections and broadcasts every create and move with the canonical card and its column id\n- [ ] 3.4 Guarantee that a move is atomic and that broadcasts reflect only committed state (no client may ever observe the same card in two columns)\n- [ ] 3.5 Handle `position` collisions or precision exhaustion by renormalizing the affected column's positions and broadcasting the corrected order\n\n## 4. Frontend Implementation\n\n- [ ] 4.1 Render columns and their cards from `GET /api/board` on initial load\n- [ ] 4.2 Implement drag-and-drop to move a card within a column and across columns\n- [ ] 4.3 On drop, optimistically reposition the card in the DOM, then send `PATCH /api/cards/:id/move`\n- [ ] 4.4 Connect to the SSE endpoint using `EventSource`; on incoming create/move events, update the DOM to the canonical state, ensuring a card never renders in more than one place\n- [ ] 4.5 Reconcile optimistic state: when the server's canonical position differs from the optimistic guess, snap the card to the server's order\n- [ ] 4.6 Let users create a card in any column from the UI (for example, a text input and an add button in each column): submitting sends `POST /api/cards` with that column's id and the text, clears the input, and shows the new card at the end of that column\n\n## Acceptance Criteria (graded)\n\n- A newly created card appears on every connected client.\n- Moving a card across columns updates all clients; the card exists in exactly one column on every client.\n- Two clients reordering cards within the same column converge to one identical order.\n- Concurrent moves of the same card by two clients leave it in exactly one place on all clients, with no duplication and no loss.\n- Reloading any client reproduces the server's exact board state.\n- Card ordering within each column is total and stable; no two cards occupy the same rendered slot.<turn|>\n<|turn>model\n<|tool_call>call:write_file{content:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"kanban-board\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"concurrently \\\"npm run dev:server\\\" \\\"npm run dev:client\\\"\",\n    \"dev:server\": \"npm run dev --prefix server\",\n    \"dev:client\": \"npm run dev --prefix client\",\n    \"install:all\": \"npm install && npm install --prefix server && npm install --prefix client\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"concurrently\": \"^8.2.2\"\n  }\n}\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>package.json<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:write_file{value:<|\"|>wrote 391 chars to package.json<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|tool_call>call:write_file{content:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"kanban-server\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"main\": \"index.js\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"@electric-sql/pglite\": \"^0.2.0\",\n    \"cors\": \"^2.8.5\",\n    \"express\": \"^4.18.2\"\n  },\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"node index.js\"\n  }\n}\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>server/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:write_file{value:<|\"|>wrote 251 chars to server/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|tool_call>call:write_file{content:<|\"|>{\n  \"name\": \"kanban-client\",\n  \"private\": true,\n  \"version\": \"0.0.0\",\n  \"type\": \"module\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"dev\": \"vite\",\n    \"build\": \"vite build\",\n    \"preview\": \"vite preview\"\n  },\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"vite\": \"^5.0.0\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"vite\": \"^5.0.0\"\n  }\n}\n<|\"|>,path:<|\"|>client/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_call|><|tool_response>response:write_file{value:<|\"|>wrote 282 chars to client/package.json<|\"|>}<tool_response|><|channel>thought\n","generated":""}
